meta {
  name: ImportPolicyFromHub
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{PROVIDER_MANAGEMENT}}/v1alpha/policyhub/policies/{{HUB_POLICY_ID}}/import
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("importedPolicyId", res.getBody()['@id']);
  bru.setEnvVar("importedPolicyName", res.getBody()['policyHubName']);
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

docs {
  Pulls a policy from the Construct-X Policy Hub into the provider control plane. The control
  plane fetches GET {hub}/api/v1/policies/{{HUB_POLICY_ID}}/odrl itself (URL and vault-based
  basic-auth credentials are configured via constructx.policyhub.* in the docker-compose.yaml)
  and stores it as a local policy definition. Repeating the request updates the definition.

  The returned "@id" is the Policy Hub's UUID (same value as HUB_POLICY_ID), not the Policy
  Hub's human-readable policy name - the stored policy definition is addressed by that UUID in
  the connector. The human-readable name (e.g. "meine-policy-date") is returned separately as
  "policyHubName", so it stays easy to tell which Policy Hub policy a given connector-side UUID
  corresponds to.

  The post-response script stores "@id" in the env var "importedPolicyId", which
  CreateContractDefinition uses for accessPolicyId/contractPolicyId - so switching
  HUB_POLICY_ID to a different hub policy and re-running this request automatically points the
  next CreateContractDefinition run at the right policy. "policyHubName" is stored in
  "importedPolicyName" purely for readability when inspecting the environment.

  Prerequisite: the Policy Hub stack (policy-hub/docker-compose.yml) is running on port 8080.
}
